1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * Reference: [https://www.myheritage.com/research/record-1-368332481-1-500883/ingebrigt-buaas-in-myheritage-family-trees?indId=externalindividual-bd7c0cdf5958618efbb042fc1ba4fba7&mrid=69165144a4bea25b6966e3bfb98ab3
2 CONC 8c MyHeritage Family Trees] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Sep 4 2022, 13:27:15 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT As an adult, Ingebrigt Larsen Buaas was an acting Fogd and later became a  merchant involved in trade. He also had minor involvement in the Bodo Affair.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is also the son of Bombard Lars Ingebrictssen Buaas. 
2 CONT
2 CONT  Why:
2 CONT 1.) The baptismal record of Ingebrigt Larsen of 1786 matches the year of birth from the Obituary for Ingebrigt Larsen Buaas in the Trondheim Newspaper.  
2 CONT 2.) Although Ingrebrigt lived in Bodo since he was 15 years old and died there--his wife sent an obituary to a Trondheim newspaper-suggesting he was from there and had family there.  
2 CONT 3.) An Ingebrigt Larsen born 1786 is living with Bombard Lars  Ingebrictssen (Ingebrigt is listed as Lars' son) in the 1801 Census.  Although he did not use Buaas--we know this is Bombard Lars Buaas.  As there are other military p
2 CONC eople living with him. Thus his son is Ingebrigt Larsen Buaas.   This is confusing since an Ingebrigt Buaas is also in the 1801 Bodo Census--(living with Joen Rygh the Fogd in Salten Norway)  After some research, it appears the Ce
2 CONC nsus in Bodo was taken after the one in Trondheim--thus he was counted twice.
2 CONT 4.) Lastly, a farm book "Innstranda i Bodo" mentions Ingebrigt's baptism in Trondheim as 4/17/1786 (which matches) and death in Bodo in 1821.  So the Trondheim Ingebrigt is the same as the Bodo Ingebrigt and  Lars' son.
